=== lesson
id: py-if
title: if, elif and else
summary: Programs make decisions with `if`: a block of code runs only when a condition is `True`. Add `else` for the other case, and `elif` to check several possibilities in order, where the first true one wins.

=== learn
### Making a decision
Until now, every line of a program ran, top to bottom. An `if` statement lets a program **choose**: some lines run only when a condition is `True`.
```python
temperature = 30
if temperature > 25:
    print("It's hot today")
print("Have a nice day")
```
```output
It's hot today
Have a nice day
```
`temperature > 25` is `True`, so the indented line runs. Change `temperature` to `18` and the condition is `False`: Python skips the indented line, and only `Have a nice day` is printed.

### The parts of an if statement
```python
if temperature > 25:
    print("It's hot today")
```
- `if` is the keyword.
- `temperature > 25` is the **condition**: anything that gives `True` or `False`, like the comparisons from the last lesson.
- The line ends with a **colon** `:`.
- The lines under it, pushed in by **4 spaces**, are the **block** (or **body**). They run only when the condition is `True`.

The block ends at the first line that is back at the left edge. That line, and everything after it, runs either way.

### Indentation decides what's inside
In Python, the spaces at the start of a line aren't decoration: they decide which lines belong to the `if`. Compare:

| Program | When `x` is 3 it prints |
| --- | --- |
| `if x > 5:` / `····print("big")` / `····print("done")` | nothing |
| `if x > 5:` / `····print("big")` / `print("done")` | `done` |

(Each `····` stands for 4 spaces.) In the first program `print("done")` is indented, so it's part of the block and is skipped too. In the second it's back at the left edge, so it always runs.

A block needs at least one line. Forgetting to indent gives an IndentationError:
```output
  File "main.py", line 3
    print("big")
    ^^^^^
IndentationError: expected an indented block after 'if' statement on line 2
```
Use the same 4 spaces for every line in a block. (In the editor on this site, Tab types 4 spaces and Enter after a colon indents the next line for you.)

### else: the other case
`else` gives a block to run when the condition is `False`:
```python
age = 15
if age >= 18:
    print("You can vote")
else:
    print("Too young to vote")
```
```output
Too young to vote
```
Exactly **one** of the two blocks runs, never both and never neither. `else` has no condition of its own (it means "otherwise"), it ends with a colon, and it lines up with its `if`.

### elif: more than two choices
`elif` (short for "else if") adds more conditions to check, in order:
```python
score = 72
if score >= 90:
    print("A")
elif score >= 80:
    print("B")
elif score >= 70:
    print("C")
else:
    print("F")
```
```output
C
```
Python checks the conditions **from the top down** and runs the block of the **first** one that is `True`. Then it skips everything else in the chain, without even checking the remaining conditions:

| Condition | With `score = 72` | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `score >= 90` | `False` | skip, check the next one |
| `score >= 80` | `False` | skip, check the next one |
| `score >= 70` | `True` | print `C`, then leave the chain |
| `else` | not checked | skipped |

`else` at the end catches everything that matched nothing above it. It's optional: without it, a chain where nothing is `True` simply runs no block.

### The order of elif matters
Because the first true condition wins, the order of the checks can change the answer:
```python
score = 95
if score >= 70:
    print("pass")
elif score >= 90:
    print("excellent")
```
```output
pass
```
`95 >= 70` is `True`, so the first block runs and the chain is over. The `excellent` branch can **never** run: every score that is at least 90 is also at least 70, so it's always caught first. Put the most specific (strictest) check first:
```python
score = 95
if score >= 90:
    print("excellent")
elif score >= 70:
    print("pass")
```
```output
excellent
```

### Separate ifs versus one chain
Two `if` statements one after another are **two separate decisions**, and both can run. One `if`/`elif` chain runs **at most one** block:
```python
n = 12
if n > 10:
    print("more than 10")
if n % 2 == 0:
    print("even")
```
```output
more than 10
even
```
If the second `if` were an `elif`, only `more than 10` would print, because the chain stops at its first true condition. Use a chain when the cases are alternatives ("which grade?"), and separate `if`s when they are independent questions ("is it big?" and "is it even?").

### Combining conditions
Any condition from the booleans lesson works after `if`, including `and`, `or`, `not` and chained comparisons:
```python
age = 15
has_ticket = True
if 13 <= age <= 19 and has_ticket:
    print("Teen ticket accepted")
if not has_ticket or age < 13:
    print("Ask an adult")
```
```output
Teen ticket accepted
```

### An if inside an if
A block can contain another `if`. The inner block is indented one more level (8 spaces in total):
```python
age = 20
member = False
if age >= 18:
    if member:
        print("Free entry")
    else:
        print("Ticket: $10")
else:
    print("Under 18s need an adult")
```
```output
Ticket: $10
```
The inner `if` is only checked when the outer condition is `True`. Nesting is useful, but two levels are usually enough; deeper code can often be flattened with `and`.

### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What Python says |
| --- | --- |
| no colon: `if x > 5` | `SyntaxError: expected ':'` |
| one `=`: `if x = 5:` | `SyntaxError: invalid syntax. Maybe you meant '==' or ':=' instead of '='?` |
| a condition on else: `else x < 5:` | `SyntaxError: expected ':'` |
| body not indented | `IndentationError: expected an indented block after 'if' statement on line ...` |
| `elif` without an `if` before it | `SyntaxError: invalid syntax` |

> Values work as conditions too: `if name:` is `True` for any non-empty string and `False` for the empty string `""`. Likewise `0` counts as `False` and every other number as `True`. This is handy, but `if name != "":` says the same thing more clearly while you're learning. It also explains the warning in the booleans lesson: `if day == "Saturday" or "Sunday":` always runs its block, because when the left side is False, the result of `or` is the right side, the non-empty string `"Sunday"`, which counts as True.

=== keypoints
- `if condition:` runs the indented block (4 spaces) only when the condition is `True`. Lines back at the left edge always run.
- `else:` runs when the condition is `False`: exactly one of the two blocks runs. `else` has no condition.
- `elif` chains are checked **top to bottom**, and only the **first** true branch runs. Put the strictest check first.
- Separate `if` statements are independent and can all run; one `if`/`elif` chain runs at most one block.
- Conditions can use `and`, `or`, `not` and chains like `13 <= age <= 19`. An `if` can sit inside another `if`'s block.
- Missing colons, `=` instead of `==`, and missing indentation are the most common errors.

=== example: Ticket prices
intro: An if/elif/else chain picks exactly one price. Change age and run it again.
```python
age = 15
if age < 5:
    price = 0
elif age < 13:
    price = 4
elif age < 65:
    price = 9
else:
    price = 5
print("Age", age, "pays", price)
```
2: Is 15 less than 5? No, so Python skips this block and moves to the next check.
4: Is 15 less than 13? No, skip again.
6: Is 15 less than 65? Yes, so this is the first true condition.
7: price is set to 9, and the rest of the chain (including else) is skipped.
8-9: else would catch ages 65 and over, but it isn't reached this time.
10: Back at the left edge, so this line always runs: Age 15 pays 9

=== example: Checking a password
intro: An if/else with two separate ifs inside the else block, so every problem gets reported.
```python
password = "sun7"
long_enough = len(password) >= 8
ends_with_digit = password[-1].isdigit()
if long_enough and ends_with_digit:
    print("Password accepted")
else:
    print("Password rejected")
    if not long_enough:
        print("- use at least 8 characters")
    if not ends_with_digit:
        print("- end with a digit")
print("Checked", len(password), "characters")
```
2: The password has 4 characters, so long_enough is False.
3: The last character is 7, a digit, so ends_with_digit is True.
4: and needs both: False and True is False, so the accepted block is skipped.
6-7: else runs instead and prints Password rejected.
8-9: An if inside the else block: not long_enough is True, so this message prints.
10-11: A separate if: not ends_with_digit is False, so this message is skipped.
12: Back at the left edge: this always runs.

=== animation: Which branch runs?
view: vars
intro: Watch the highlighted line jump. Python checks each condition in order and runs only the first true branch.
```python
score = 78
if score >= 90:
    grade = "A"
elif score >= 80:
    grade = "B"
elif score >= 70:
    grade = "C"
else:
    grade = "F"
print("Grade:", grade)
```
1: score will hold 78.
2: First check: is {score} >= 90? That's {score >= 90}, so Python skips this branch's block.
4: Next check: is {score} >= 80? {score >= 80} again, so skip.
6: Is {score} >= 70? {score >= 70}! This is the first true condition.
7: Run this branch's block: grade becomes "C". The else is never checked.
10: The chain is finished, and this line is outside it. It prints Grade: {grade}

=== animation: When order goes wrong
view: vars
intro: The same kind of chain with the checks in the wrong order. Watch which branch catches a score of 95.
```python
score = 95
if score >= 50:
    result = "pass"
elif score >= 90:
    result = "excellent"
else:
    result = "fail"
print(result)
```
1: score will hold 95, which should be excellent.
2: The first check is {score} >= 50, which is {score >= 50}. The first true condition wins...
3: ...so result becomes "pass", and the rest of the chain is skipped.
8: The excellent branch was never even checked. Every score that's at least 90 is also at least 50, so this chain can never give excellent. The strictest check has to come first.

=== task: mcq easy
title: Spot the correct if
prompt: Which of these lines is a correct start of an if statement?
- `if x > 5` :: The colon at the end is missing. Python gives `SyntaxError: expected ':'`.
* `if x > 5:` :: Right: the keyword, a condition, and a colon at the end.
- `if x = 5:` :: One `=` stores a value; it can't be a condition. Comparing needs `==`.
- `If x > 5:` :: Python is case-sensitive, so the keyword must be `if` in small letters.
hint: Check three things: the keyword's spelling, the condition, and the end of the line.
hint: Every line that opens a block ends with the same character.
right: An `if` line is the keyword `if` (small letters), a condition that gives `True` or `False`, and a colon. The colon tells Python that an indented block comes next.
wrong: A correct `if` line has `if` in small letters, a real condition (comparing uses `==`, not `=`), and a colon at the end.

=== task: predict easy
title: Hot or not?
code:
```python
temp = 30
if temp > 25:
    print("hot")
print("done")
```
hint: Is the condition `True` or `False` when `temp` is 30?
hint: Which line is outside the if block?
right: `30 > 25` is `True`, so the indented `print("hot")` runs. `print("done")` is back at the left edge, so it runs either way.
wrong: The condition `30 > 25` is `True`, so the block runs and prints `hot`. Then `print("done")`, which isn't indented, runs no matter what.
wrong[hot]: `print("done")` is not indented, so it's not inside the if. It always runs, after the if is finished.
wrong[done]: `30 > 25` is `True`, so the indented line runs too, before `done`.

=== task: predict easy
title: Even or odd?
code:
```python
n = 7
if n % 2 == 0:
    print("even")
else:
    print("odd")
print("checked", n)
```
hint: What is `7 % 2`?
hint: When the condition is `False`, the else block runs instead.
right: `7 % 2` is `1` (the remainder), so `n % 2 == 0` is `False`. The else block runs and prints `odd`, then the last line always runs.
wrong: `7 % 2` gives the remainder 1, which isn't 0, so the condition is `False` and the else block runs: `odd`. The last line is outside both blocks, so it runs too.
wrong[even\nchecked 7]: `7 % 2` is 1, not 0, so the condition is `False`. That sends Python to the else block.
wrong[odd]: The last line isn't indented, so it isn't part of the else. It always runs and prints `checked 7`.
wrong[even\nodd\nchecked 7]: An if/else runs exactly one of its two blocks, never both.

=== task: code easy
title: Free or paid?
prompt: Children under 5 get in free, and everyone else pays. `age` is already set, and the checks try several ages. Use an if/else to set `ticket` to `"free"` when `age` is under 5, and to `"paid"` otherwise.
starter:
```python
# age is already set before your code runs
# your code here
```
solution:
```python
if age < 5:
    ticket = "free"
else:
    ticket = "paid"
```
tests:
- ticket
cases:
- age = 3
- age = 5
- age = 4
- age = 30
fail[1]: A 3-year-old is under 5, so `ticket` should be 'free'.
fail[2]: Age 5 isn't under 5, so it should be 'paid'. "Under 5" is `age < 5`; `age <= 5` would let 5-year-olds in free.
error[NameError]: Make sure `ticket` gets a value in **both** branches, and spell `age` and `ticket` exactly like this.
error[SyntaxError]: Check that the `if` line and the `else` line both end with a colon, and that `else` has no condition.
error[IndentationError]: The line under `if` and the line under `else` must each be indented by 4 spaces.
require[if]: Use an if statement.
require[else]: Use `else` for everyone who isn't under 5.
hint: The condition is "age is under 5": `age < 5`.
hint: One branch sets `ticket = "free"`, and the `else:` branch sets `ticket = "paid"`.
hint: Write `if age < 5:`, an indented `ticket = "free"`, then `else:` and an indented `ticket = "paid"`.
right: The condition `age < 5` is True for 3 and 4, so their tickets are free. For 5 and 30 it's False, so the else block runs instead. Exactly one of the two blocks runs, so `ticket` always gets a value.
wrong: Write an if/else: `if age < 5:` with `ticket = "free"` indented under it, then `else:` with `ticket = "paid"` indented under it. Use `<`, because a 5-year-old pays.

=== task: predict easy
title: When the condition is False
code:
```python
balance = 20
if balance < 0:
    print("overdrawn")
print("balance:", balance)
```
wrong[overdrawn\nbalance: 20]: `20 < 0` is False, so the indented line is skipped. Only the line back at the left edge runs.
wrong[balance: 20\noverdrawn]: Lines run from top to bottom, and the indented line is skipped anyway, because `20 < 0` is False.
hint: Is `20 < 0` True or False?
hint: Which line is outside the if block?
right: `20 < 0` is False, so Python skips the indented `print("overdrawn")`. `print("balance:", balance)` is back at the left edge, so it runs either way.
wrong: The condition `20 < 0` is False, so the block is skipped and nothing is printed from it. The last line isn't indented, so it always runs: `balance: 20`.

=== task: mcq easy
title: Which line always runs?
prompt: `rain` holds `True` or `False`. Which line runs **whatever** `rain` holds?
code:
```python
if rain:
    print("Take an umbrella")
    print("Wear boots")
print("Go outside")
```
- `print("Take an umbrella")` :: It's indented under the `if`, so it runs only when `rain` is True.
- `print("Wear boots")` :: It's indented by 4 spaces like the line above it, so it's part of the same block. It runs only when `rain` is True.
* `print("Go outside")` :: Right. It's back at the left edge, so it's outside the block and always runs.
- None of them :: The last line isn't inside the if, so it doesn't depend on `rain` at all.
hint: The indentation decides which lines belong to the if.
hint: The block ends at the first line that's back at the left edge.
right: The two indented lines make up the block, and they run only when `rain` is True. `print("Go outside")` is back at the left edge, so it's after the if and runs every time.
wrong: Every line indented under the `if` belongs to its block, so both of the first two prints depend on `rain`. The block ends at `print("Go outside")`, which is back at the left edge and always runs.

=== task: fill easy
title: The missing keyword
prompt: Fill the blank so that a 12-year-old gets the child ticket:
```output
Child ticket
```
code:
```python
age = 12
if age >= 13:
    print("Teen ticket")
??:
    print("Child ticket")
```
answer: else
wrong[elif]: `elif` needs a condition of its own, like `elif age < 13:`. On its own, `elif:` is a SyntaxError. The keyword that needs no condition is `else`.
wrong[if]: `if` always needs a condition, so `if:` is a SyntaxError. To run a block when the first condition is False, use `else`.
hint: You need the keyword that means "otherwise".
hint: It's the one keyword in a chain that never has a condition.
right: `else:` runs its block when the `if` condition is False. `12 >= 13` is False, so `Child ticket` prints.
wrong: The blank is followed directly by a colon, so it has no condition, and only `else` works like that. It runs when `age >= 13` is False, which it is for 12.

=== task: cells easy
title: Hot or not, three times
prompt: For each value of `temp`, what does this code print?
code:
```python
if temp > 25:
    print("hot")
else:
    print("not hot")
```
labels: temp = 30 | temp = 25 | temp = 10
answer: hot | not hot | not hot
wrong[hot | hot | not hot]: `25 > 25` is False: `>` doesn't include the equal case. So 25 goes to the else block: not hot.
wrong[not hot | not hot | not hot]: `30 > 25` is True, so the if block runs for 30 and prints hot.
hint: Put each value into the condition `temp > 25` and decide True or False.
hint: `>` doesn't include the equal case.
right: 30 > 25 is True (hot). 25 > 25 is False, because `>` doesn't count equal, so the else runs (not hot). 10 > 25 is False (not hot).
wrong: Test each value: 30 > 25 → True → hot. 25 > 25 → False → not hot. 10 > 25 → False → not hot.

=== task: parsons easy
title: Too cold for ice cream
prompt: Arrange the lines so that the warning prints only when it's below 15 degrees, and the goodbye always prints. With `temp = 12` the program prints:
```output
Too cold for ice cream
Have a nice day
```
lines:
```python
temp = 12
if temp < 15:
    print("Too cold for ice cream")
print("Have a nice day")
```
distractors:
```python
if temp < 15
if temp = 15:
```
hint: The if line needs a colon at the end, and it compares with `<`.
hint: Only the warning goes inside the block. The goodbye goes back at the left edge.
right: `12 < 15` is True, so the indented warning runs. `print("Have a nice day")` sits at the left edge, outside the block, so it would print even on a warm day.
wrong: Set `temp`, then `if temp < 15:` (with its colon), then the warning indented under it, then the goodbye back at the left edge. `if temp < 15` without the colon is a SyntaxError, and `=` stores a value instead of comparing.

=== task: predict easy
title: Traffic light
code:
```python
light = "yellow"
if light == "green":
    print("go")
elif light == "yellow":
    print("slow down")
else:
    print("stop")
```
wrong[slow down\nstop]: Only one branch of a chain runs. Once `light == "yellow"` is True, the else is skipped.
wrong[stop]: `light == "yellow"` is True, so that branch runs and the else is never reached.
wrong[go\nslow down]: `light == "green"` is False ("yellow" isn't "green"), so its block is skipped.
hint: Check the conditions from the top, one at a time.
hint: The first True condition wins, and the rest of the chain is skipped.
right: `"yellow" == "green"` is False, so Python checks the elif: `"yellow" == "yellow"` is True, so it prints `slow down`. The chain is then finished, and the else is skipped.
wrong: Go down the chain: the green check is False, the yellow check is True, so `slow down` prints, and nothing else in the chain runs.

=== task: predict medium
title: A chain and a separate if
code:
```python
x = 15
if x > 10:
    print("big")
elif x > 5:
    print("medium")
if x > 12:
    print("over 12")
else:
    print("12 or less")
```
hint: Lines 2 to 5 are one chain. Line 6 starts a new, separate if statement.
hint: In a chain only the first true branch runs, even if a later condition is also true.
right: The first chain stops at its first true condition, `x > 10`, so only `big` prints from it (`medium` is skipped even though `15 > 5` is also true). The second if is a separate decision: `15 > 12` is `True`, so it prints `over 12`.
wrong: There are two separate decisions. The first chain prints only `big`, because a chain runs only its first true branch. The second `if` starts fresh: `15 > 12` is `True`, so it prints `over 12`.
wrong[big\nmedium\nover 12]: `elif` belongs to the first chain, and a chain runs only its first true branch. `x > 10` was true, so the `medium` branch is skipped.
wrong[big]: Line 6 starts a new `if`, which isn't part of the first chain. It's checked on its own: `15 > 12` is `True`, so `over 12` prints.
wrong[big\n12 or less]: `x` is 15, and `15 > 12` is `True`, so the second if prints `over 12`.

=== task: parsons medium
title: Positive, negative or zero
prompt: Arrange lines into a program that prints whether `x` is positive, negative or zero. With `x = -3` it should print:
```output
negative
```
Use one if/elif/else chain, and indent the bodies (use the indent buttons). Not every line is needed.
lines:
```python
x = -3
if x > 0:
    print("positive")
elif x < 0:
    print("negative")
else:
    print("zero")
```
distractors:
```python
elif x = 0:
else x < 0:
```
hint: The chain starts with `if`, then `elif`, then `else`, each followed by its own indented print.
hint: `else` never has a condition, and comparing uses `==`, not `=`.
right: The chain checks `x > 0` (false), then `x < 0` (true, so it prints `negative`). `else` needs no condition, because it catches the only case left: zero.
wrong: Order the chain as if, elif, else, with each print indented under its own line. The distractors are broken: `else` can't have a condition, and `x = 0` stores a value instead of comparing.

=== task: fill medium
title: Build the chain
prompt: Fill the three blanks with the keywords `if`, `elif` and `else` so that this is **one** chain, and only one grade is printed. With `score = 95` it should print:
```output
A
```
code:
```python
score = 95
?? score >= 90:
    print("A")
?? score >= 80:
    print("B")
??:
    print("C")
```
answer: if | elif | else
hint: A chain starts with one keyword, continues with another, and ends with a third.
hint: The last line has no condition, so it can only be one keyword.
right: `if` starts the chain, `elif` adds a second condition to the **same** chain, and `else` (no condition) catches everything else. With 95, the first branch prints `A` and the rest of the chain is skipped.
wrong: A chain is `if` → `elif` → `else`. Only `if` can start it, `elif` continues it, and `else` ends it without a condition.
wrong[if | if | else]: Two `if`s make two separate decisions. 95 is both `>= 90` and `>= 80`, so this prints `A` and then `B`. Using `elif` joins the second check to the chain, so it's skipped once `A` has been printed.
wrong[elif | elif | else]: A chain can't start with `elif`: there's no `if` for it to continue, so Python reports `SyntaxError: invalid syntax`.
wrong[if | else | elif]: `else` must come last and has no condition. Here it has a condition, `score >= 80`, which is a SyntaxError.

=== task: cells medium
title: Grade four scores
prompt: For each score, which letter does this code print?
code:
```python
if score >= 90:
    print("A")
elif score >= 80:
    print("B")
elif score >= 70:
    print("C")
else:
    print("F")
```
labels: score = 95 | score = 80 | score = 79 | score = 45
answer: A | B | C | F
wrong[A | C | C | F]: 80 is caught by `score >= 80`, because `>=` includes 80 itself: `80 >= 80` is `True`. So it's a B.
wrong[A | B | B | F]: 79 isn't `>= 80`, so the B branch is skipped. The next check, `79 >= 70`, is true: C.
wrong[A | B | C | C]: 45 fails every condition, so none of the if/elif blocks run, and the else catches it: F.
hint: For each score, go down the chain and stop at the first condition that's `True`.
hint: `>=` includes the equal case: `80 >= 80` is `True`.
right: 95 stops at the first check (A); 80 fails `>= 90` but passes `>= 80` (B); 79 first passes at `>= 70` (C); 45 fails every condition, so else catches it (F).
wrong: Test each score against the conditions in order and stop at the first true one. Remember `>=` includes the boundary, so 80 is a B and 79 is a C.

=== task: code medium
title: Price a ticket
prompt: Set `price` according to `age`:
- under 5: `0`
- 5 to 12: `4`
- 13 to 64: `9`
- 65 and over: `5`

`age` is already set before your code runs, and the checks try several different ages, so don't set `age` yourself. Use one if/elif/else chain.
starter:
```python
# age is already set: write the chain that sets price
price = 0
```
solution:
```python
if age < 5:
    price = 0
elif age < 13:
    price = 4
elif age < 65:
    price = 9
else:
    price = 5
```
tests:
- price
cases:
- age = 3
- age = 5
- age = 12
- age = 13
- age = 40
- age = 64
- age = 65
- age = 90
fail[2]: Age 5 should cost 4. "Under 5" means `age < 5`, so 5 itself is not free.
fail[3]: Age 12 should cost 4: the second band is 5 to 12. Check it with `age < 13` (or `age <= 12`).
fail[4]: Age 13 should cost 9. If 13 got 4, your second check includes 13: use `age < 13`.
fail[6]: Age 64 should cost 9; only 65 and over pay 5. Use `age < 65` for the third band.
fail[7]: Age 65 should cost 5. Check that your third band stops before 65 (`age < 65`), so 65 falls through to the else.
error[NameError]: Don't create or rename `age`: it's already set before your code runs. Make sure you create `price` in every branch.
hint: Check the bands from youngest to oldest: `if age < 5:` first.
hint: Because the chain stops at the first true condition, `elif age < 13:` only sees ages that are 5 or more.
hint: After `elif age < 65:` the only ages left are 65 and over, so `else:` handles them.
right: Each condition only has to check the upper limit, because the earlier branches already caught the younger ages: by the time `age < 13` is checked, `age` is known to be at least 5. That's what makes an elif chain so tidy.
wrong: Check the bands in order with one chain: `if age < 5`, `elif age < 13`, `elif age < 65`, `else`. Each branch sets `price`. The boundaries matter: 5, 13 and 65 belong to the higher band.

=== task: code medium
title: Ice, water or steam
prompt: Water is ice at 0 degrees or below, steam at 100 degrees or above, and water in between. `temp` is already set, and the checks try several temperatures. Use one if/elif/else chain to set `state` to `"ice"`, `"water"` or `"steam"`.
starter:
```python
# temp is already set before your code runs
# your code here
```
solution:
```python
if temp <= 0:
    state = "ice"
elif temp < 100:
    state = "water"
else:
    state = "steam"
```
tests:
- state
cases:
- temp = -5
- temp = 0
- temp = 1
- temp = 99
- temp = 100
- temp = 150
fail[2]: At exactly 0 degrees it's ice, because "0 or below" includes 0. Use `temp <= 0`.
fail[4]: 99 degrees is still water. If you got steam, check your second condition: water is everything below 100.
fail[5]: At exactly 100 degrees it's steam, because "100 or above" includes 100. So the water branch must stop before 100: `temp < 100`.
error[NameError]: Make sure `state` gets a value in every branch, and spell `temp` and `state` exactly like this.
error[SyntaxError]: Check the colons at the end of the `if`, `elif` and `else` lines, and that `else` has no condition.
require[elif]: Use one if/elif/else chain.
hint: Start with the coldest case: `if temp <= 0:`.
hint: The elif only sees temperatures above 0, so it only needs the upper limit: `elif temp < 100:`.
hint: Everything left is 100 or more: `else:` sets `"steam"`.
right: The chain checks the coldest case first. By the time `temp < 100` is checked, the temperature is known to be above 0, so that branch is exactly the water range, and the else catches 100 and above. The boundaries 0 and 100 land where the rules say.
wrong: Use three branches in order: `if temp <= 0:` → "ice", `elif temp < 100:` → "water", `else:` → "steam". `<=` puts 0 itself in the ice branch, and `<` sends 100 itself to the steam branch.

=== task: multi medium
title: Two separate decisions
prompt: Choose **every** statement about this program that is true.
code:
```python
if score >= 50:
    print("pass")
if score >= 80:
    print("merit")
else:
    print("try again")
```
* With `score = 90`, it prints `pass` and then `merit`. :: Right. The two ifs are separate decisions, and both conditions are True for 90.
* With `score = 60`, it prints `pass` and then `try again`. :: Right. 60 passes the first if. The second if is a separate decision with its own else: 60 >= 80 is False, so its else runs.
* With `score = 30`, it prints only `try again`. :: Right. The first if is skipped. The second if is False too, so its else runs.
- The `else` belongs to the first `if`. :: An else belongs to the `if` right above it at the same indentation, which is the second one, `if score >= 80:`.
- The program never prints more than one line. :: Two separate ifs can both run. Only one if/elif/else chain is limited to one block.
hint: Line 3 starts a new if statement. Which if does the else belong to?
hint: Try each score: work through the first if, then the second if with its else. Three statements are true.
right: These are two independent decisions. The first if prints `pass` for 50 and over. The second if/else always prints exactly one line: `merit` for 80 and over, and `try again` otherwise, even for a score like 60 that passed.
wrong: An else belongs to the nearest if above it at the same indentation, here `if score >= 80:`. So the program makes two separate decisions: score 90 → pass, merit; 60 → pass, try again; 30 → try again.

=== task: order medium
title: Follow the chain
prompt: `x` is 7. Put the steps in the order Python carries them out when it runs this code:
```python
if x > 10:
    print("big")
elif x > 5:
    print("medium")
else:
    print("small")
print("done")
```
items:
- Check `x > 10`: it's False, so skip that block
- Check `x > 5`: it's True
- Run that block: print `medium`
- Leave the chain without checking the `else`
- Print `done`
hint: Python checks the conditions from the top down.
hint: After the first True condition's block runs, the rest of the chain is skipped. The last line is outside the chain.
right: The chain is checked from the top: `7 > 10` is False, `7 > 5` is True, so `medium` prints and the chain ends without looking at the else. Then the line after the chain, `print("done")`, runs.
wrong: Go top to bottom: first `x > 10` (False, skip), then `x > 5` (True), then its block prints `medium`. That ends the chain, so the else is never checked. Last, `print("done")` runs because it's outside the chain.

=== task: predict medium
title: An if inside an if
code:
```python
x = 8
if x > 5:
    if x > 10:
        print("A")
    else:
        print("B")
    print("C")
else:
    print("D")
print("E")
```
wrong[B\nE]: `print("C")` is indented by 4 spaces, so it's in the outer block, after the inner if/else. It runs whenever `x > 5` is True.
wrong[B\nC\nD\nE]: The outer condition `8 > 5` is True, so the outer else is skipped: no D.
wrong[A\nC\nE]: The inner condition is `8 > 10`, which is False, so the inner else runs: B.
hint: Start with the outer condition. Then work through everything indented under it, in order.
hint: `print("C")` has 4 spaces, the same as the inner `if`. Which block is it in?
right: `8 > 5` is True, so the outer block runs. Inside it, `8 > 10` is False, so the inner else prints `B`. Then `print("C")`, which is in the outer block after the inner if/else, prints `C`. The outer else is skipped, and `E` is outside everything.
wrong: The outer condition is True, so its block runs: the inner if/else prints B (8 > 10 is False), then the outer block's last line prints C. The outer else (D) is skipped, and E always prints.

=== task: predict medium
title: Empty text and zero
code:
```python
name = ""
if name:
    print("Hello", name)
else:
    print("No name given")
count = 0
if count:
    print("count is set")
print("end")
```
wrong[Hello\nend]: An empty string counts as False in a condition, so the else runs: No name given.
wrong[No name given\ncount is set\nend]: 0 counts as False in a condition, so the second if's block is skipped.
wrong[No name given]: The second if has no else, so it prints nothing when its condition is False, but `print("end")` is outside it and always runs.
hint: Used as a condition, the empty string `""` counts as False, and so does the number 0.
hint: The second if has no else.
right: `if name:` treats the empty string as False, so the else prints `No name given`. `if count:` treats 0 as False, so its block is skipped, and with no else nothing else prints. Then `end` always prints.
wrong: Values can be conditions: `""` and `0` count as False, and any other string or number counts as True. So the first if/else prints `No name given`, the second if prints nothing, and the last line prints `end`.

=== task: predict hard
title: Nested decisions
code:
```python
